# The scratch image builds the binary INSIDE itself (docker/scratch.Dockerfile), so the build context
# is the source tree — and these are the parts of it that must not travel.
#
# `.git` is excluded, which has a visible consequence rather than none: `KoreBuildIdentity` stamps
# `commit = unknown` in that image. It already does on the Linux box, whose mutagen replica carries
# no `.git` either. The alternative is worse than it looks — a file git TRACKS but `.dockerignore`
# excludes reads as deleted inside the build, and the stamp becomes `-dirty` for ever.
.git
.github
build
*/build
# ...EXCEPT the one artefact the runtime image is made of. `docker/native.Dockerfile` copies the
# linked binary out of `server/build/`, and the two lines above — added for the scratch image, which
# builds its binary inside itself and must not be handed a stale one — silently made that COPY fail
# with "not found". Docker evaluates these in order and the last match wins, so the exception below
# re-includes a single file out of an excluded directory. Nothing noticed for a day because the
# `xyk:dev` tag from before the exclusion was still in the local daemon: a broken build and a
# working image look identical until something rebuilds.
!server/build/bin/native/releaseExecutable/server.kexe
.gradle
.kotlin
.idea
docs
